**Read:** [Refining of Mineral Oil and Gas, BREF REF](../sources/Mineral-Oil-and-Gas-Refining-BREF.lingua#r_3ZQ3XT8F2XTED0CF8KN79K9NP6), the complete general feed, assay/preparation and primary fractionation chapters; [Organic Chemical Technology — Nanda Kishore, NPTEL](../sources/Organic-Chemical-Technology-Kishore.lingua#r_1ERYESBGNKCKMDY0NPHJKCVJG0), the whole petroleum/refinery-products and characteristics sequence; [Chemical Technology I — Indra Deo Mall, NPTEL](../sources/Chemical-Technology-I-Mall.lingua#r_2QDGEANAX8QTGRS531GFVF4H74), the full petroleum/feed and associated industrial-process teaching where available.

Crude oil and natural gas are mixtures whose composition and properties vary with source and treatment history. A bulk name or density cannot specify all boiling fractions, sulfur/nitrogen compounds, metals, water, salts, reactive constituents or phase behavior needed for process decisions.

An assay and a set of cuts are models supported by particular measurements. Pseudocomponents can make a complex mixture tractable, but their grouping and property assignments influence subsequent calculations. Distinguish laboratory characterization, an assumed blend and the feed actually delivered over time.

Preparation and fractionation create streams with different chemical roles. Removing water/salt or conditioning a gas can protect downstream equipment/catalysts and alter residual/utility accounts. A physical separation does not create every desired molecular product; chemical conversion may be required later.

**Work:** reconstruct a complete public feed assay/preparation account with its sampling and measurement methods. Compare two feeds or blends at a defined product slate, independently checking material, impurity and energy consequences. Explain which property-model and assay uncertainties most influence the result and what observations would test a consequential pseudocomponent assumption.

